WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.304
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:08.909
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:17.213
Unit twenty-seven: Operations Management. Today you will describe services arranged for a company with causative have / get.

4
00:00:17.513 --> 00:00:20.073
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:24.371
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.217 --> 00:00:26.520
Operations.

7
00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:31.931
Operations means the day-to-day activities that produce goods or services.

8
00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:34.992
Operations run from 6 a.m. to midnight.

9
00:00:35.583 --> 00:00:38.096
Your turn. Say: Operations.

10
00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:42.120
Operations.

11
00:00:42.883 --> 00:00:44.059
Efficiency.

12
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:48.512
Efficiency means achieving maximum output with minimum waste.

13
00:00:49.117 --> 00:00:52.475
New machines improved efficiency by 15%.

14
00:00:53.083 --> 00:00:55.898
Your turn. Say: Efficiency.

15
00:00:58.733 --> 00:00:59.827
Efficiency.

16
00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:01.584
Process.

17
00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:05.825
Process means a series of steps taken to achieve a result.

18
00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:08.892
The ordering process now takes three clicks.

19
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:12.635
Your turn. Say: Process.

20
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:16.664
Process.

21
00:01:17.417 --> 00:01:18.186
Quality.

22
00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:22.263
Quality means the standard of something compared with others.

23
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.128
Quality checks happen at every stage.

24
00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:28.009
Your turn. Say: Quality.

25
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:31.786
Quality.

26
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:33.679
Inventory.

27
00:01:34.683 --> 00:01:37.786
Inventory means the stock of goods a business holds.

28
00:01:38.383 --> 00:01:40.615
They count inventory every Saturday.

29
00:01:41.217 --> 00:01:44.357
Your turn. Say: Inventory.

30
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:48.329
Inventory.

31
00:01:49.083 --> 00:01:50.038
Bottleneck.

32
00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:54.837
Bottleneck means a point where work slows down and causes delays.

33
00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.856
The packing station was the main bottleneck.

34
00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.940
Your turn. Say: Bottleneck.

35
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:04.743
Bottleneck.

36
00:02:05.483 --> 00:02:06.601
Productivity.

37
00:02:07.617 --> 00:02:10.893
Productivity means the amount produced per worker or per hour.

38
00:02:11.483 --> 00:02:14.447
Productivity rose after the layout changed.

39
00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.900
Your turn. Say: Productivity.

40
00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:21.827
Productivity.

41
00:02:22.583 --> 00:02:23.445
Lean.

42
00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:27.239
Lean means focused on eliminating waste.

43
00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:30.314
Lean methods cut waiting time in the clinic.

44
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:33.325
Your turn. Say: Lean.

45
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:37.063
Lean.

46
00:02:37.867 --> 00:02:41.645
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:42.483 --> 00:02:49.446
Visal, the noodle factory in Kandal can't meet demand. The owner wants to buy more machines. Is that the answer?

48
00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:58.488
Not yet. First he should have his processes reviewed. An expert found the real bottleneck was packing, not cooking.

49
00:02:59.583 --> 00:03:01.653
So the new machines would be wasted?

50
00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:11.161
Probably. He got the packing line redesigned for $3,000, and output rose 20% without a single new machine.

51
00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:13.576
What's his next step?

52
00:03:14.683 --> 00:03:21.547
He's having his inventory system digitised, so ingredients never run out on busy days.

53
00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.595
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.216
Grammar focus: Causative have and get.

55
00:03:28.516 --> 00:03:29.749
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.682
The company had its processes reviewed.

57
00:03:33.583 --> 00:03:35.745
He got the packing line redesigned.

58
00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:39.381
We are having our inventory system digitised.

59
00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:51.670
Use 'have/get + object + past participle' when someone else does a service for you. Word order matters: We had our accounts reviewed (a service) ≠ We had reviewed our accounts (we did it ourselves).

60
00:03:52.417 --> 00:03:56.799
Be careful! Don't say: We had reviewed our accounts by an expert.

61
00:03:58.733 --> 00:04:01.499
Say: We had our accounts reviewed by an expert.

62
00:04:01.799 --> 00:04:04.379
Have + object + past participle.

63
00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:07.935
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:08.733 --> 00:04:10.025
Finding the Bottleneck.

65
00:04:10.325 --> 00:04:18.547
Operations management focuses on turning inputs such as labour, materials and energy into goods and services as efficiently as possible.

66
00:04:19.533 --> 00:04:25.295
A central insight is that a system can only move as fast as its slowest step, known as the bottleneck.

67
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:34.186
Lean thinking, developed by Toyota, encourages firms to eliminate waste such as waiting, excess inventory and unnecessary movement.

68
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.659
Many small businesses improve dramatically simply by having their workflow mapped and analysed by an outsider.

69
00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:47.358
Productivity gains therefore often come from smarter processes rather than from larger investments in equipment.

70
00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:52.584
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.403
We had the machine blank yesterday.

72
00:04:55.703 --> 00:05:00.355
A, repair. B, repaired. C, repairing.

73
00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:05.958
The answer is B: repaired.

74
00:05:07.117 --> 00:05:09.592
A point where work slows down is a blank.

75
00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:13.522
A, bottleneck. B, process. C, quality.

76
00:05:17.067 --> 00:05:19.031
The answer is A: bottleneck.

77
00:05:20.183 --> 00:05:23.112
The amount produced per worker or per hour is blank.

78
00:05:23.412 --> 00:05:27.773
A, inventory. B, efficiency. C, productivity.

79
00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:34.041
The answer is C: productivity.

80
00:05:35.183 --> 00:05:37.171
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:37.471 --> 00:05:44.573
A, She got her laptop fixed.. B, She got fixed her laptop.. C, She got her laptop fix..

82
00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:51.189
The answer is A: She got her laptop fixed..

83
00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.673
According to the reading, a system moves only as fast as…

84
00:05:55.973 --> 00:06:00.555
A, its newest machine. B, its slowest step. C, its manager.

85
00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.761
The answer is B: its slowest step.

86
00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:11.176
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:12.017 --> 00:06:14.550
How can a small business work more efficiently?

88
00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:32.769
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:33.069 --> 00:07:08.202
A small business can work more efficiently by identifying where time and money are wasted. First, the owner could have the whole process mapped from order to delivery, which often reveals a single bottleneck. Second, simple tools such as digital inventory records or online ordering can remove repetitive manual work. For example, a bakery might discover that bakers spend an hour daily searching for ingredients. Finally, staff should be asked for ideas, because they see problems that managers miss.

90
00:07:08.502 --> 00:07:10.177
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:11.083 --> 00:07:15.303
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and causative have and get.

92
00:07:15.603 --> 00:07:19.370
Today's value is thoroughness. Do the whole job, and do it well.

93
00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:22.392
See you in Unit twenty-eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:22.692 --> 00:07:25.379
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

